WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.225
Welcome to Part 2 of the implementation
series for provider notices.

2
00:00:04.225 --> 00:00:09.054
As in Part 1, one thing first: Provider
Notices, An Implementation Guide,

3
00:00:09.054 --> 00:00:14.050
is a draft, awaiting the owner's review,
so check it again after that review.

4
00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:19.477
Part 1 collected four providers into
one record: Azure Service Health,

5
00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:24.062
Microsoft 365, Oracle Cloud
Infrastructure and AWS Health.

6
00:00:24.062 --> 00:00:25.774
Part 2 finishes the loop.

7
00:00:25.774 --> 00:00:28.144
Gate five adds Google Cloud's incidents,

8
00:00:28.144 --> 00:00:30.800
and records the gap for
planned maintenance.

9
00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:34.859
Gate six routes every notice
that needs action to an owner,

10
00:00:34.859 --> 00:00:36.888
as a task, without duplicates.

11
00:00:36.888 --> 00:00:40.132
Gate seven writes the weekly
digest the owners read,

12
00:00:40.132 --> 00:00:43.300
and the evidence chain the
security officer keeps.

13
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.000
Then how to run it safely:
control states and change rings,

14
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.488
the dangers, the evidence file,
rollback, and what couldn't be verified.

15
00:00:51.488 --> 00:00:57.350
The kit's code is illustrative, tested
against fixtures, not against a live tenant.

16
00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:05.775
Here's where Part 1 left off, on the kit's
fixtures with every provider enabled.

17
00:01:05.775 --> 00:01:11.481
One collection gives twenty-two notices:
five from Azure, six from Microsoft 365,

18
00:01:11.481 --> 00:01:15.890
four from Oracle Cloud, five from
AWS and two from Google Cloud,

19
00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.175
and every one is valid
against the same schema.

20
00:01:19.175 --> 00:01:24.291
Each record says what kind of notice it
is, its window, the resources it names,

21
00:01:24.291 --> 00:01:28.087
and whether action is required,
by when, and on whose word.

22
00:01:28.087 --> 00:01:32.013
What the collection doesn't give you
yet is the part that closes the gap.

23
00:01:32.013 --> 00:01:36.198
Nobody owns these notices until
each one that needs action has an

24
00:01:36.198 --> 00:01:40.074
owner and a due date, and a second
run mustn't make a second task.

25
00:01:40.074 --> 00:01:42.850
The owners need something
to read every week.

26
00:01:42.850 --> 00:01:46.850
And the security officer needs
evidence that can't be quietly edited.

27
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:48.562
That's gates six and seven.

28
00:01:48.562 --> 00:01:50.650
First, Google Cloud.

29
00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.803
Gate five is Google Cloud's
Personalized Service Health,

30
00:01:57.803 --> 00:02:01.712
which lists the incidents relevant
to a project or organization.

31
00:02:01.712 --> 00:02:04.899
Google's documentation
sets one hard limit:

32
00:02:04.899 --> 00:02:08.175
the API has a single
event category, incident.

33
00:02:08.175 --> 00:02:12.314
Planned maintenance isn't in it, so
this gate covers incidents only,

34
00:02:12.314 --> 00:02:15.400
and the gap is recorded
with the security officer.

35
00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:18.260
The project owner enables the API once and

36
00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.038
grants the reader's group the viewer role.

37
00:02:21.038 --> 00:02:24.551
Then cross-check with Google's
own list command, and collect.

38
00:02:24.551 --> 00:02:25.917
For an organization,

39
00:02:25.917 --> 00:02:30.100
the settings say so and the role
is granted on the organization.

40
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.594
But the gcloud flags for
organization events weren't read,

41
00:02:33.594 --> 00:02:37.525
so the kit marks that call unverified
and runs it only when told to.

42
00:02:37.525 --> 00:02:41.841
And for government workloads: Google's
Assured Workloads page lists this

43
00:02:41.841 --> 00:02:46.087
product under regional packages, but
not under FedRAMP Moderate or High.

44
00:02:46.087 --> 00:02:49.630
A workload in those packages
has nothing to read here.

45
00:02:53.380 --> 00:02:56.342
On the fixtures, Google
Cloud looks like this.

46
00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:59.068
The kit declares two calls
with the viewer role.

47
00:02:59.068 --> 00:03:02.742
The project call is verified
against Google's documentation.

48
00:03:02.742 --> 00:03:05.602
The organization call
says verified, false,

49
00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:08.304
which is why it runs
only when you allow it.

50
00:03:08.304 --> 00:03:12.377
The collection gives two incidents,
one open and one closed,

51
00:03:12.377 --> 00:03:17.530
and none needs action: Google's API has
no field that says action is required.

52
00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.962
The records show where the
products and locations come from,

53
00:03:20.962 --> 00:03:24.668
the event's impacts, which is what
the guide's validate step checks.

54
00:03:24.668 --> 00:03:29.579
Cloud SQL in one region is open;
Cloud Storage in another is closed.

55
00:03:29.579 --> 00:03:30.955
And that's the whole gate.

56
00:03:30.955 --> 00:03:33.780
It supports the same two
controls as gate one,

57
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:37.293
monitoring the provider and
receiving its advisories.

58
00:03:37.293 --> 00:03:42.130
Rollback is simply removing the role
binding when the kit is retired.

59
00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.430
Gate six turns notices into work.

60
00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:53.667
A notice that needs action becomes a task
with an owner; the rest go in the digest.

61
00:03:53.667 --> 00:03:58.082
First, the routing is written at
work, inside the agency's boundary,

62
00:03:58.082 --> 00:04:01.843
from the kit's template, with
its prompts in Copilot Chat.

63
00:04:01.843 --> 00:04:04.771
Rules are tried in order
and the first match wins,

64
00:04:04.771 --> 00:04:10.042
so security advisories and retirements come
before the general action-required rule.

65
00:04:10.042 --> 00:04:13.987
Second, check it: the kit
refuses an unknown match key,

66
00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:18.667
a task rule with no severity or
group, and a placeholder left in.

67
00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:20.842
Third, route as a dry run.

68
00:04:20.842 --> 00:04:22.931
Each task is shaped for ServiceNow,

69
00:04:22.931 --> 00:04:27.322
with a severity and a response window
from the program's finding contract,

70
00:04:27.322 --> 00:04:29.554
the provider's own date kept separate,

71
00:04:29.554 --> 00:04:33.081
and the five clauses of the
Ticket-to-Machine request chain.

72
00:04:33.081 --> 00:04:36.380
Fourth, send once the
agency's endpoint exists.

73
00:04:36.380 --> 00:04:41.980
And a task closes only on the re-read, never
because the provider closed the notice.

74
00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.505
Here's routing on the fixtures.

75
00:04:47.505 --> 00:04:50.080
Day one's twenty-two notices are all new.

76
00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:54.381
Twelve route to a task, nine are
digest only and one is ignored,

77
00:04:54.381 --> 00:04:58.855
so the dry run writes twelve
create payloads and sends nothing.

78
00:04:58.855 --> 00:05:00.788
You can see the routing working:

79
00:05:00.788 --> 00:05:04.504
the security notifications go
to security operations at P2,

80
00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:08.815
the retirements and maintenance to
cloud platform engineering at P3,

81
00:05:08.815 --> 00:05:11.417
and the Azure incident
to the service desk.

82
00:05:11.417 --> 00:05:12.943
Then the important test.

83
00:05:12.943 --> 00:05:16.871
Route the same collection again,
against a copy of the state,

84
00:05:16.871 --> 00:05:20.880
and every notice is unchanged,
so no payload is written at all.

85
00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:25.307
That's what the guide's validate step
asks for: every security advisory and

86
00:05:25.307 --> 00:05:30.243
retirement routes to a task, and a second
run on the same collection writes nothing.

87
00:05:30.243 --> 00:05:34.600
The provider hasn't changed the
notice, so nobody gets a second task.

88
00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.675
A week later, the providers have moved.

89
00:05:40.675 --> 00:05:44.363
Day two's routing finds two
new notices, six changed,

90
00:05:44.363 --> 00:05:47.525
fifteen unchanged and
one no longer listed.

91
00:05:47.525 --> 00:05:50.775
So it writes one create,
four updates and one close,

92
00:05:50.775 --> 00:05:53.312
and nothing for the fifteen unchanged.

93
00:05:53.312 --> 00:05:57.039
The updates are two rescheduled
maintenance windows,

94
00:05:57.039 --> 00:06:02.175
an updated Microsoft 365 post, and an
incident the provider no longer lists.

95
00:06:02.175 --> 00:06:06.144
The close is the AWS security
notification the provider closed,

96
00:06:06.144 --> 00:06:10.562
and that close payload asks for the
re-read rather than closing the task.

97
00:06:10.562 --> 00:06:14.117
Below it is the request chain
on the Azure retirement's task,

98
00:06:14.117 --> 00:06:15.713
in the Human decision lane.

99
00:06:15.713 --> 00:06:17.626
Clause one says where it came from.

100
00:06:17.626 --> 00:06:20.550
Two names the approver
role, the system owner.

101
00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.313
Three is the action, under change control.

102
00:06:23.313 --> 00:06:25.800
Four is the re-read check that closes it.

103
00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:28.330
And five says what evidence is kept.

104
00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:33.443
What does routing support?

105
00:06:33.443 --> 00:06:35.368
Three things in NIST's catalog.

106
00:06:35.368 --> 00:06:38.361
Getting advisories to the
people who act on them,

107
00:06:38.361 --> 00:06:41.729
and acting within set time
frames or reporting the gap.

108
00:06:41.729 --> 00:06:45.071
Configuration change
control and impact analysis,

109
00:06:45.071 --> 00:06:48.955
so a change a provider announces
is reviewed before it lands.

110
00:06:48.955 --> 00:06:52.710
And contingency planning, because
retirements and maintenance

111
00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:56.117
that name the agency's resources
prompt a review of the plan.

112
00:06:56.117 --> 00:06:58.886
The security officer
keeps the task payloads,

113
00:06:58.886 --> 00:07:01.217
the routing file's output and the state.

114
00:07:01.217 --> 00:07:05.408
One honest note from FedRAMP's
pages: no key security indicator

115
00:07:05.408 --> 00:07:09.518
cites the base impact-analysis
control, only its enhancement.

116
00:07:09.518 --> 00:07:14.084
Routing calls no cloud at all, so there's
no government-cloud question here;

117
00:07:14.084 --> 00:07:16.780
the ServiceNow endpoint
is the agency's own.

118
00:07:16.780 --> 00:07:20.993
Until it exists, the service desk
works from the dry run's files.

119
00:07:20.993 --> 00:07:25.250
And rollback is reverting the routing
file, while keeping the state.

120
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:32.163
Gate seven makes it a weekly
habit and proves it ran.

121
00:07:32.163 --> 00:07:35.051
The digest is what the
owners read every week;

122
00:07:35.051 --> 00:07:38.175
the evidence is what the
security officer keeps.

123
00:07:38.175 --> 00:07:42.530
The deployment runbook takes both to a
test environment and then production,

124
00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:46.000
with the same commands and one
settings file per environment.

125
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:47.125
Three steps.

126
00:07:47.125 --> 00:07:51.474
First, a preflight that only reads:
the settings and routing files,

127
00:07:51.474 --> 00:07:56.629
each provider's tool and signed-in cloud,
the reader's first call, and for AWS the

128
00:07:56.629 --> 00:07:59.287
support plan, the named notice contacts,

129
00:07:59.287 --> 00:08:02.187
and in production the
test stage's evidence.

130
00:08:02.187 --> 00:08:04.805
Second, the weekly run: collection,

131
00:08:04.805 --> 00:08:09.012
routing and the digest, each
step recorded as evidence.

132
00:08:09.012 --> 00:08:11.249
Third, the go or no-go.

133
00:08:11.249 --> 00:08:14.849
It needs a passing record for
every step the stage lists,

134
00:08:14.849 --> 00:08:20.012
with production adding the alert policy,
an unbroken chain, and matching hashes.

135
00:08:20.012 --> 00:08:24.140
The scheduler is the agency's;
the kit doesn't register one.

136
00:08:27.890 --> 00:08:31.615
Here's gate seven on the
fixtures, after routing both days.

137
00:08:31.615 --> 00:08:35.796
The digest shows sixteen open
notices, eight needing action,

138
00:08:35.796 --> 00:08:41.315
five starting in the next thirty days, two
new, and four closed or no longer listed.

139
00:08:41.315 --> 00:08:44.580
Every action-required notice
shows its due date and the

140
00:08:44.580 --> 00:08:47.915
group it was routed to, which
is the guide's pass condition.

141
00:08:47.915 --> 00:08:49.153
Then the evidence.

142
00:08:49.153 --> 00:08:51.950
Each run folder becomes a numbered record:

143
00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:55.627
the collection, the routing
and the digest, each passing.

144
00:08:55.627 --> 00:08:58.859
The check confirms that
every file matches the hash

145
00:08:58.859 --> 00:09:01.940
list and the three records
form an unbroken chain.

146
00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:05.152
And watch what happens when you
check them as the test stage.

147
00:09:05.152 --> 00:09:08.711
The chain still passes, but
the preflight and the scheduled

148
00:09:08.711 --> 00:09:12.415
run have no passing record, because
on a laptop nobody ran them.

149
00:09:12.415 --> 00:09:16.252
So the go or no-go fails, and
the script exits with an error.

150
00:09:16.252 --> 00:09:19.930
That's the point: the stage
can't pass on a partial run.

151
00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:25.280
What does gate seven support?

152
00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.970
Each week's digest supports getting
advisories to the people who act,

153
00:09:28.970 --> 00:09:31.560
monitoring the providers,
and updating the risk

154
00:09:31.560 --> 00:09:34.667
assessment when the environment
changes significantly.

155
00:09:34.667 --> 00:09:39.857
The evidence chain supports continuous
monitoring and the protection of audit

156
00:09:39.857 --> 00:09:44.892
information: each record holds the time,
the environment, the change record, the

157
00:09:44.892 --> 00:09:46.984
command, the result, the controls,

158
00:09:46.984 --> 00:09:50.005
every file's hash, and the
previous record's hash.

159
00:09:50.005 --> 00:09:53.586
And the preflight's check that each
provider's notice contacts are

160
00:09:53.586 --> 00:09:57.492
named and current supports NIST's
control for notification agreements.

161
00:09:57.492 --> 00:09:59.105
Here's why the chain matters.

162
00:09:59.105 --> 00:10:03.842
In the workbook's lab, someone appends a
line to the digest after it was recorded.

163
00:10:03.842 --> 00:10:07.958
The next check fails twice: the file
no longer matches the hash list,

164
00:10:07.958 --> 00:10:10.129
and it no longer matches its record.

165
00:10:10.129 --> 00:10:14.160
Nobody can quietly edit the
evidence an assessor will read.

166
00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:20.576
Before the dangers, how
the guide states itself

167
00:10:20.576 --> 00:10:23.447
against the workbook's
control-state conventions.

168
00:10:23.447 --> 00:10:26.611
Provider notices are a
detective, operating loop.

169
00:10:26.611 --> 00:10:30.273
They inform owners and open
tasks, and they block nothing.

170
00:10:30.273 --> 00:10:34.384
So the design is the owners, the
routing and the notice contacts.

171
00:10:34.384 --> 00:10:38.635
The configuration is a reader per
provider holding only its read role.

172
00:10:38.635 --> 00:10:43.073
Enforcement doesn't apply, except
G1's alert policy at scope.

173
00:10:43.073 --> 00:10:46.884
The evidence is each gate's
validate step and the stage check.

174
00:10:46.884 --> 00:10:51.773
And operation is the weekly run, with
tasks closed only on the re-read.

175
00:10:51.773 --> 00:10:56.060
Change safety, which is design
guidance, runs in three rings.

176
00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:00.960
Ring zero is the test stage, which
exits when its evidence check passes.

177
00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:05.018
Ring one is production with
collection and dry-run routing only,

178
00:11:05.018 --> 00:11:07.622
until the owners have
read a week's digest.

179
00:11:07.622 --> 00:11:11.490
Ring two applies the alert
policy and turns on sending.

180
00:11:15.240 --> 00:11:17.665
Nine dangers, each with its guard.

181
00:11:17.665 --> 00:11:22.753
Never act on a notice from email alone:
attackers send look-alike provider mail,

182
00:11:22.753 --> 00:11:26.824
so act only on the notice the kit
read from the provider's own API.

183
00:11:26.824 --> 00:11:31.990
Read security advisories without the
elevated role and you miss their details.

184
00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:37.084
Read from the wrong cloud and you get nothing,
and the gate passes on an empty list,

185
00:11:37.084 --> 00:11:40.402
so every cloud is named and
the kit refuses any other.

186
00:11:40.402 --> 00:11:45.075
Don't close a task because the provider
closed the notice: a retirement can close

187
00:11:45.075 --> 00:11:48.049
while the resource still
runs the retired product,

188
00:11:48.049 --> 00:11:49.890
so the close asks for the re-read.

189
00:11:49.890 --> 00:11:52.952
Don't assume Google maintenance
is covered; it isn't.

190
00:11:52.952 --> 00:11:57.603
AWS without a support plan reads
nothing, and the preflight catches it.

191
00:11:57.603 --> 00:12:00.178
Hand-edited alert rules
can be overwritten.

192
00:12:00.178 --> 00:12:03.802
The agency's routing never
goes in a public repository.

193
00:12:03.802 --> 00:12:08.510
And if the weekly run stops silently,
the evidence chain shows the gap.

194
00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:15.454
Each gate's table names what
the security officer keeps,

195
00:12:15.454 --> 00:12:17.260
and together they make one file.

196
00:12:17.260 --> 00:12:20.888
The collection gates keep
each provider's raw response,

197
00:12:20.888 --> 00:12:24.122
the notices and a manifest,
with Azure's alert plan.

198
00:12:24.122 --> 00:12:28.122
Routing keeps the task payloads,
the routing output and the state.

199
00:12:28.122 --> 00:12:30.851
And the digest gate
keeps the weekly digests,

200
00:12:30.851 --> 00:12:34.760
the preflight results and the
evidence chain with its hash list.

201
00:12:34.760 --> 00:12:39.874
The controls run from monitoring external
providers and receiving their advisories,

202
00:12:39.874 --> 00:12:43.851
through change control, impact
analysis and contingency planning,

203
00:12:43.851 --> 00:12:46.408
to risk assessment, continuous monitoring,

204
00:12:46.408 --> 00:12:50.172
protection of audit information
and notification agreements.

205
00:12:50.172 --> 00:12:54.860
The kit's records support those controls;
the agency's assessor decides them.

206
00:12:54.860 --> 00:12:58.831
The guide's evidence checklist
has five parts: the configuration,

207
00:12:58.831 --> 00:13:03.980
the weekly operational files, each test
result, the exceptions, including providers

208
00:13:03.980 --> 00:13:09.130
not enabled and Google's maintenance gap,
and the evidence chain for the period.

209
00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:16.793
Rollback is short, because
almost everything only reads.

210
00:13:16.793 --> 00:13:20.662
For Azure, plan and apply the
deletion of the policy assignment,

211
00:13:20.662 --> 00:13:24.680
and let the change record decide
on what its remediation created.

212
00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:27.489
Gates two to five have
nothing to roll back;

213
00:13:27.489 --> 00:13:31.180
when the kit is retired, remove
each reader's permission.

214
00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.030
Routing reverts to the last
reviewed file and keeps its state.

215
00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:40.368
And the digest gate unregisters the schedule
and keeps every run and its evidence.

216
00:13:40.368 --> 00:13:43.493
The guide is also candid
about what it couldn't verify.

217
00:13:43.493 --> 00:13:47.600
Several Azure details aren't stated
on Learn, including Resource

218
00:13:47.600 --> 00:13:51.706
Graph's full column schema and the
alert policy's parameter names.

219
00:13:51.706 --> 00:13:54.680
Learn doesn't enumerate
the message center tags.

220
00:13:54.680 --> 00:13:57.598
Oracle's CLI output keys
and its third realm,

221
00:13:57.598 --> 00:14:01.893
and the gcloud flags for organization
events, weren't confirmed.

222
00:14:01.893 --> 00:14:05.754
The ServiceNow side and the
scheduler are the agency's to build.

223
00:14:05.754 --> 00:14:08.570
And nothing in the guide
was run in a tenant.

224
00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:14.670
Here's what done looks like, gate by gate.

225
00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:20.090
Every Azure subscription read by a Reader,
security advisories by the elevated role,

226
00:14:20.090 --> 00:14:23.033
and the alert policy
assigned and remediated.

227
00:14:23.033 --> 00:14:27.482
Microsoft 365 read on the
tenant's own Graph host.

228
00:14:27.482 --> 00:14:33.008
Every Oracle tenancy read, latest in
each chain, with affected resources.

229
00:14:33.008 --> 00:14:36.178
Every AWS account with a
qualifying support plan,

230
00:14:36.178 --> 00:14:38.420
read from its partition's endpoint.

231
00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.833
Google's incidents read, and
the maintenance gap recorded.

232
00:14:41.833 --> 00:14:46.171
The routing reviewed, every notice
that needs action with a task,

233
00:14:46.171 --> 00:14:50.182
no duplicates on a re-run, and
tasks closed only on the re-read.

234
00:14:50.182 --> 00:14:54.158
And the weekly run scheduled,
the digest reaching the owners,

235
00:14:54.158 --> 00:14:57.258
and each stage's evidence
passing the go or no-go.

236
00:14:57.258 --> 00:14:59.695
To build it, use the developer workbook,

237
00:14:59.695 --> 00:15:02.819
Building Provider Notices,
and its training path.

238
00:15:02.819 --> 00:15:05.257
And one last time: this guide is a draft,

239
00:15:05.257 --> 00:15:09.345
awaiting the owner's review, so
check it again after that review.

240
00:15:09.345 --> 00:15:10.160
Thank you.
